Subject: Oceania vs. Cyberspatial Libertaria

Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com> said:
> Hmm. Sorry: I'd like to believe in this, but current events in the UK
> seem to suggest that governments can and will respond effectively to
> this kind of threat.
> 
> A case in point is the rave culture; another case is the culture of 
> New Age traveller convoys that troll around the English countryside.
> The response the the UK government has been draconian and
> illuminating.

< More stuff deleted >

> The point is, TAZ's are _temporary_. Because as soon as they are seen
> to exist and to represent a threat, the state _WILL_ clamp down on
> them. Not might. Not could. But Will.

The obvious problem for the travellers is that they were/are not trying to
keep a low profile. In many (if not most) cases they were squatting on 
someone's land without permission, and regardless of your opinions about 
land ownership, if you do that currently then the owner is going to call in 
the police eventually to kick you off. In addition to which, most of the big 
landowners in this country have probably made large donations to the Tory party 
in the past and expect some hippy-bashing in return.

So I think you're right - if you want your TAZ to last a reasonable length
of time, you have to make sure that you're *not* seen to exist. That's why
most people interested in avoiding the government are attempting to do
so via anonymity (e.g. the 3-4% of the UK population who vanished at the 
last census, the poll-tax evaders who didn't register on the electoral roll, 
etc).

With regard to the travellers in particular, have these draconian measures
actually had any effect on them ? I haven't met any for a year or two, but from 
what I've heard, their number is still growing, they're just staying away
from the South-East. So, while the government may have been successful in
keeping the travellers away from Tory voters, they don't actually seem to
have had much success in their attempts to destroy their lifestyle (probably
far less than the success they've had at destroying the lifestyle of Tory
voters through their economic mismanagement). 

If anything, their economic policies have probably increased the number of 
travellers by putting more people out of work, increasing house repossessions, 
etc....

			Mark

