Date: Thu, 18 Nov 1993 14:19:39 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: QUERY: NP Completeness

Simon! Levy:

> I won't feel complete until someone explains NP completeness to me.

It's not very complicated.  Suppose we have some computational problem.
This problem will take some input.  Let the size of that input be S.
We run an algorithm to solve the problem, producing an answer.  This
takes some number of computational steps N(S).  We are interested in how
N(S) increases as S increases.  If there is some polynomial function
(of any fixed degree!) F such that there is an algorithm for the problem
where N(S) <= F(S), we say that the problem is solvable with a polynomial
time algorithm.  Let's call the set of all such problems 'P'.

An example of a problem not in P is to compute the expansion of
f(x) = 2^2^x, which will take an exponential amount of time just
to store the answer.  Note that here S = log(x).

Let's define another set of problems, called 'NP'.  NP problems
have the property that it must be possible to confirm that an answer
is in fact an answer in polynomial time (so the above example
not-P problem would not be in NP either).  Factoring a large integer X
is an example of an NP problem, because clearly we can just mulitply
the primes in the solution together to verify that the solution is
correct, which is a polynomial-time process.

So it should be clear that a problem in P is also in NP.  But what about
the other way around?  Are NP problems in P?  That is, does P = NP?
Nobody knows for sure, but most everybody believes that the answer is no.

An "NP-complete" problem is one that can have a polynomial time
algorithm if and only if P = NP.  This means that *any* NP problem can
be converted into an NP-complete problem in polynomial time -- so
if a polynomial time solution is found to an NP-complete problem,
a polynomial time solution would exist for all NP problems, and
P would equal NP.  Proving that a problem is NP-complete can
be pretty arcane.

derek


