which gives me an opportunity to recommend the book THE NEW TURING
OMNIBUS by A. K. Dewdney.  This book contains 66 (I think) sections,
each on some aspect of C.S. or C.S. related mathematics, each one to
three pages or so in length.

One section is on NP-Completeness, all sections are very readable and
informative.

Problems are in class P if there are polynomial time algorithms for
solving them, i.e., a problem is in P if there exists some turing
machine which takes no more than O(x^n) steps to solve the problem, for
some fixed integer n, where x is the length of the problem's
representative input string.

A problems is in class NP (which stands for Nondeterministic Polynomial
time, not "non-polynomial time") if there is a nondeterministic
automaton which solves it in polynomial time.  A nondeterministic
automaton is an automaton which, instead of choosing one computational
path and renouncing another (like an if statement) , proceeds along all
paths in parallel.  Sort of like the multifurcating universe in the many
worlds (EWG) interpretation of quantum mechanics.  Then the number of
steps required to solve the problem by the nondeterministic machine is
the number of steps taken by whichever path is lucky enough to get to
the solution of the problem first.

An outstanding conjecture of Computer Science these days is, P=NP?

There is an equivalence class of problems in NP called NP-complete
problems.  As I recall, the circuit-satisfiability problem and the
Hamiltonian cycle problem are both in the class NP-complete.  The
interesting feature of NP-complete is the relation among its members:
it can be shown that if any member of NP-complete is in P, then
every member of NP-complete must also be in P.  So, since there are
some pretty hard problems in NP-complete which people have been trying
to find polynomial time algorithms for (which people have been trying
to put in P) for a long time now, showing that some new problem is
in NP-complete means it is probably very difficult, it is probably
very hard to find a poly-time alogrithm for it.

Hmmm... now I seem to remember something about ((NP-complete in P) =>
P=NP)?  I have no suitable textbook handy to refresh my memory.

Anyway, I do recommend the Dewdney book.

I hope this helps!  (I hope I have not misremembered anything important
and made a fool of myself...)

